item.content:encoded:
As promised, the last one (for now?), and this one blew my mind the first
time I saw it:    var_v0 = sp4014;
    if ((s32) sp4014 < 0) {
        var_v0 = sp4014 + 0x7FF;
    }
    temp_s1 = 0x800 - (sp4014 - (((s32) var_v0 >> 0xB) << 0xB));
There's actually two things going on here. First, the compiler
is faking a signed division using shifting (which rounds down) as a building block:
If the value is negative, then you add a bias to make shift into round-up.
(The right-shift by 0xB, or 11, is exactly a division by 0x800.)So this means what we're looking at is:    var_v0 = (s32) sp4014 / 0x800;
    temp_s1 = 0x800 - (sp4014 - var_v0 * 0x800);
which reduces further to:    temp_s1 = 0x800 - ((s32) sp4014 % 0x800);
Tada. That's it. Now you know all the tricks, except those that I don't know
or that I forgot. You don't need the LLM. Have fun. :-)Edit: Corrected a thinko; of course, MIPS has signed division.
This is for power-of-two optimizations only.

Occasionally, you'll see code like this (after initial cleanup):    var_v0_2 = crc & 0x80000000;
    do {
       if (var_v0_2 != 0) {
           crc = (crc << 1) ^ 0x1EDC6F41;
       } else {
           crc <<= 1;
       }
       var_a0 += 1;
       var_v0_2 = crc & 0x80000000;
    } while (var_a0 < 8U);
The thing with  is unnatural. GCC sometimes does these loop rotation tricks, seemingly
to save registers (don't ask me how exactly it does). You want to un-rotate
back into:    do {
       var_v0_2 = crc & 0x80000000;
       if (var_v0_2 != 0) {
           crc = (crc << 1) ^ 0x1EDC6F41;
       } else {
           crc <<= 1;
       }
       var_a0 += 1;
    } while (var_a0 < 8U);
and then of course, you can clean up the rest:for (i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
    if (crc & 0x80000000) {
        crc = (crc << 1) ^ 0x1EDC6F41;
    } else {
        crc <<= 1;
    }
}
Last one tomorrow, I think.

We're nearing the end soon, I promise. Here's one that I see all the time:     ...
         } else {
             var_t0_5 = temp_a0_3 - 0x140;
             var_a2_5 = 0xA0 - temp_a3_5;
             goto block_45;
         }
     } else {
         var_t0_5 = 0xA0 - temp_a0_3;
         var_a2_5 = temp_a3_5 - 0x118;
block_45:               
         var_v0_3 = var_t0_5 + 0xA0;
         var_v1_4 = var_a2_5 + 0x78;
     }
So, eh, what's that goto doing there? What happened was that the compiler
helpfully figured it could help with some tail deduplication; someone
wrote the same code twice, and the compiler figured taking the cost
of a branch was better than keeping it duplicated. (In a sense, it's
the opposite of inlining. Ish.) In most cases, it's as simple as
copying the block and then you can remove the label:     ...
         } else {
             var_t0_5 = temp_a0_3 - 0x140;
             var_a2_5 = 0xA0 - temp_a3_5;
             var_v0_3 = var_t0_5 + 0xA0;
             var_v1_4 = var_a2_5 + 0x78;
         }
     } else {
         var_t0_5 = 0xA0 - temp_a0_3;
         var_a2_5 = temp_a3_5 - 0x118;
         var_v0_3 = var_t0_5 + 0xA0;
         var_v1_4 = var_a2_5 + 0x78;
     }
Sometimes, these are nested, with multiple tails goto-ing into each other,
so in that case, you'll need to reduplicate multiple times.
Make sure you actually come out on the correct side afterwards,
though.As an extra complication, the code is deduplicated not on the C statement level, but on
RTL instructions, so if you're calling functions with five parameters
of more (which requires pushing parameters onto the stack), m2c can
get really confused and start throwing  at you on the
receiving side because it's not clear at all where the parameters
would be coming from.

Yet another way of writing for loops:    var_a2_4 = ...
    if (var_a2_4 < 0x78) {
        ...
        do {
            ...
            var_a2_4 += 1;
        } while (var_a2_4 < 0x78);
    }
This extra if is merely there because the compiler wasn't able to prove
that the variable didn't start below the bound, so it can't rewrite
the while-do to a do-while without that extra check. So the rewrite
still reads:for (var_a2_4 = ...; var_a2_4 < 0x78; ++var_a2_4) {
    ...
}
possibly with the initializer skipped, if it comes from something
complex earlier.

Let's look at the entire loop from yesterday:    u16 spE10;
    u16 *var_v0_2;

    ...

    var_t1_2 = 0x7F;
    var_v0_2 = &spE10 + 0xFE;
    do {
        *var_v0_2 = 0;
        var_t1_2 -= 1;
        var_v0_2 -= 2;
    } while (var_t1_2 >= 0);
What's going with the pointer arithmetic here? Why is it indexing
out-of-bounds?The first thing to notice is that m2c doesn't really take into account
that arithmetic on pointers in C is multiplied by the size of the element.
And obviously, spE10 is the root of some array. So corrected, this reads:    u16 spE10[0x80];  // Modified.
    u16 *var_v0_2;

    ...

    var_t1_2 = 0x7F;
    var_v0_2 = spE10 + 0x7F;  // Modified.
    do {
        *var_v0_2 = 0;
        var_t1_2 -= 1;
        var_v0_2 -= 1;  // Modified.
    } while (var_t1_2 >= 0);
We saw yesterday how we should rewrite the iteration back to a for loop,
but also, most likely, the pointer arithmetic here came from ; the compiler took a multiplication operation and rewrote it
to a “weaker” operation (subtraction) since that's typically cheaper,
at the cost of making the values dependent, an easy trade. So we can
rewrite it back into multiplication:    var_t1_2 = 0x7F;
    do {
        var_v0_2 = spE10 + var_t1_2;
        *var_v0_2 = 0;
        var_t1_2 -= 1;
    } while (var_t1_2 >= 0);
and now it's easy to see that the loop should read:    for (i = 0; i < 0x80; ++i) {
        spE10[i] = 0;
    }
and then, of course, the array should get a better name.Sometimes the loop counter will go backwards while the pointer will go
forwards, which is also completely normal, especially if there's side
effects. The compiler will reverse one but not the other.

This is more of a note to myself since I keep forgetting and "rediscovering" this technique every time I need it, but if it helps you, great!The JavaScript way is to toggle displaying the menu based on a button click. For instance, here's an example in Svelte:[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]<script lang="ts">
	let showMenu = $state(false);
</script>

<button onclick={() => (showMenu = !showMenu)}>
	{#if showMenu}
		Hide menu
	{:else}
		Show menu
	{/if}
</button>
<nav class:hidden={!showMenu}>
	<ul>
		<li>Home</li>
		<li>Blog</li>
		<li>Foo</li>
		<li>Bar</li>
	</ul>
</nav>

<style>
	.hidden {
		display: none;
	}
</style>But there is a hack to use a hidden checkbox and some CSS to achieve the same thing without any JavaScript. The example here turns the menu button into a label that toggles the checkbox, and uses the checkbox state to determine when to show the menu via the next-sibling combinator.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]<input type="checkbox" id="menu-state" />
<div class="menu-ctn">
	<label class="menu-toggle" for="menu-state">
		<span class="menu-closed">Show menu</span>
		<span class="menu-open">Hide menu</span>
	</label>
	<nav class="menu-open">
		<ul>
			<li>Home</li>
			<li>Blog</li>
			<li>Foo</li>
			<li>Bar</li>
		</ul>
	</nav>
</div>

<style>
	#menu-state {
		display: none;
	}
	.menu-open {
		display: none;
	}
	.menu-closed {
		display: block;
	}
	#menu-state:checked + .menu-ctn .menu-open {
		display: block;
	}
	#menu-state:checked + .menu-ctn .menu-closed {
		display: none;
	}
</style>

Of course, you could just use the modern choice. Here, I've shortened what Schade uses into one line of CSS (because I can). As noted, this is a fairly new feature so not every browser supports this.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.].underline-me {
	text-decoration: underline red solid 3px;
}Since some browsers don't support , here are the alternatives I could think of.The workaround Schade describes is using , though it has a gap between the text in the line.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am <span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span> within the sentence..underline-me {
	border-bottom: red solid 3px;
}Probably the better way is to create an  element, which gets very close, but displays above the text (covering the letters 'g' and 'y').[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am <span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span> within the sentence..underline-me {
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me::after {
	content: '';
	position: absolute;
	width: 100%;
	height: 3px;
	background-color: red;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
}If you use a container (in this case, two nested s instead of just one), then you can get very close with the underline displaying below the text.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am
<span class="underline-me-ctn">
	<span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span>
</span>
within the sentence..underline-me-ctn {
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me {
	z-index: 10;
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me-ctn::after {
	content: '';
	position: absolute;
	width: 100%;
	height: 3px;
	background-color: red;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
}::after in a container with text strokeAnd now if you add a stroke to the text via , you're pretty much there, though this may not be feasible in some situations (e.g. gradient or image background behind the text). Support for  is not that much better than  though, so make of that what you will.[This interactive/preview is only available on the website.]I am
<span class="underline-me-ctn">
	<span class="underline-me-outline">underlining this yap</span>
	<span class="underline-me">underlining this yap</span>
</span>
within the sentence..underline-me-ctn {
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me-outline {
	z-index: 5;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	color: var(--background);
	-webkit-text-stroke: 0.3em var(--background);
}
.underline-me {
	z-index: 10;
	position: relative;
}
.underline-me-ctn::after {
	content: '';
	position: absolute;
	width: 100%;
	height: 3px;
	background-color: red;
	left: 0;
	bottom: 2px;
}
